    "ls|LS17_S1_U_172": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF INFORMATION AND BROADCASTING \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.172 \n(TO BE ANSWERED ON 21.06.2019) \n \nSUPERSTITIONS IN TV PROGRAMME \n172.  SHRI ASHOK KUMAR RAWAT: \n         Will the Minister of INFORMATION AND BROADCASTING be pleased to state: \n(a) whether many serials based on black magic, hypocrisy and superstitions are \nbeing broadcast on Doordarshan and private channels;  \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof along with the reasons therefor; and  \n \n(c) the steps taken or likely to be taken by the Government to stop the broadcast of \nsuch serials? \n \n                                                               ANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE; AND MINISTER \nOF INFORMATION AND BROADCASTING (SHRI PRAKASH JAVADEKAR) \n \n(a) to (c ): The Cable Television Network s Rules, 1994 inter -alia provides that TV \nchannels shall not carry any programme which encourages superstition or blind \nbelief.  All TV channels including Doordarshan are required to conform to these \nRules.  No serial based on Black Magic, hypocrisy and sup erstitions are being \nbroadcast on Doordarshan .  The Government has from  time to time issue  advisory \nand warn ings to private TV channels who are  found to have violated the said \nProgramme Code. \n \n                                               *********************** \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S1_U_173": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF WOMEN AND CHILD DEVELOPMENT \n \nLOK SABHA \nADMITTED UNSTARRED QUESTION NO.173 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21.06.2019 \n \nDECLINING ADOPTION RATE \n \n173.  SHRIMATI MEENAKASHI LEKHI: \n \nWill the Minister of WOMEN AND CHILD DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government has taken note of the declining adoption rates in the \ncountry; \n(b) if so, the details thereof and reasons therefor; and \n(c) the steps being taken to make the adoption procedure more transparent and \naccessible? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF WOMEN AND CHILD DEVELOPMENT \n(SHRIMATI SMRITI ZUBIN IRANI) \n \n(a) & (b) : As per the Child Adoption Resource Information and Guideline System \n(CARINGS), the adoptions of children under the Juvenile Justice (Care and Protection \nof Children) Act, 2015 shows an increasing trend. Details are as under:  \n \nYear No. of adoption  \n2016-2017 3788 \n2017-2018 3927 \n2018-2019 4027 \n(c)  Adoption Regulations, 2017 have been notified by Central Adoption Resource \nAuthority under the provision of the Juvenile Justice Care & Protection Act, 2015. The \nentire adoption procedure has been made on -line through the Child Adoption \nResource Information and Guideline System (CARINGS) which has made the system \nfully transparent and accessible. Further, the Government also undertakes awareness \ninitiatives for this purpose.  \n***** ",
    "ls|LS17_S1_U_174": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \n MINISTRY OF LAW AND JUSTICE \nLEGISLATIVE DEPARTMENT \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.174 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON WEDNESDAY, THE 3RD JULY, 2019 \nEntry of women in Sabarimala  Temple \n*174.  DR. SHASHI THAROOR: \n���������������������LAW AND JUSTICE����������������������\n�������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n������������� ���������� ��� ���������� ��� ���� �������������� ��� ��������� ����\n���������� ��� ���� �������� ������ ��� ������ ��� ������� ������ ��������\n������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n���� ��� ����� ������ �������� ������ ��� ���� ����� ��� ������ ���� �����������\n��������\n���������������������������������������\n�������������������������������������\n \nA N S W E R \n \nMINISTER OF LAW AND JUSTICE, COMMUNICATIONS AND \nELECTRONICS & INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY \n \n(SHRI RAVI SHANKAR PRASAD) \n \n(a)�to (c):  The matter is sub-judice before ���������������� Supreme Court. �\n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \n \n \n \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S1_U_175": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nDEPARTMENT OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.175\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21ST JUNE,2019\nWELLNESS CENTRES FOR FISHERMEN AND FARMERS\n175. SHRI H. VASANTHAKUMAR:\nWill the Minister of HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE be pleased to state:\n(a) whether the Government proposes to setup National Wellness Centres for Farmers and\nFishermen across the country with free medical facility;\n(b) if so, the details thereof and steps being taken by the Government in this regard;\n(c) the time by which the Government is likely to setup National Wellness Centre for Farmers\nand Fishermen and the steps taken to provide round the clock emergency medical facility across\nthe country; and\n(d) if so, details of funds allocated to initiate this setup?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HEALTH AND\nFAMILY WELFARE\n(SHRI ASHWINI KUMAR CHOUBEY)\n(a) to (d): As per the budget announcement 2017 -18, 1.5 lakh Health Sub Centres and\nPrimary Health Centres are being transformed into Health and Wellness Centres (HWCs) for\nprovision of comprehensive primary care that includes preventive and health promotion at the\ncommunity level with continuum of care approach across the country for all population including\nFarmers and Fishermen.\nThe AB -HWCs will provide an expanded range of services to include care for non -\ncommunicable diseases, palliative and rehabilitative care, Oral, Eye and ENT care, mental health\nand first level care for em ergencies and trauma as well as Health promotion and wellness\nactivities like Yoga apart from services already being provided for Maternal and Child Health\nincluding immunization and communicable diseases.\nSo far, approvals for 52,744 Ayushman Bharat - Health and Wellness Centres (AB -\nHWCs) have been accorded and as reported by the States/UTs, 18,705 Health & Wellness\nCentres are operationalized till 31st May, 2019.\n\n“Public Health & Hospitals” being a State subject, the primary responsibility of provision\nof healthcare including emergency medical facilities is that of respective State Government.\nHowever, under NHM, support is being provided to States/UTs for strengthening their\nhealthcare system including emergency healthcare on the basis of proposals received from them\nin their Programme Implementation Plans (PIPs).",
    "ls|LS17_S1_U_176": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF WOMEN AND CHILD DEVELOPMENT \n  \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.176 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21.06.2019 \n \nCORPORAL PUNISHMENT IN CHILD CARE INSTITUTIONS \n 176. SHRI ASADUDDIN OWAISI:  \nWill the Minister of WOMEN AND CHILD DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n(a) whether the analysis of data on children homes gathered between 2016 -17 has shown that nearly \n4130 child care institutions in the country have admitted to have used corporal punishme nt and \nhumiliation as measure to discipline children who are violators of law and if so, the details thereof;  \n(b) whether the Government has conducted any analysis of data in this regard during the years 2017 -\n18 and 2018-19 and if so, the details thereof; \n(c) whether the recommendations of the committee set up for the examination of Child Care Institutions \nhave been reviewed and implemented by the Government;  \n(d) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor;  \n(e) whether Government has issued any advisory to States especially after Muzaffarpur case in Bihar; \nand  \n(f) if so, the steps taken or being taken by the Government on the recommendations of the committee \nin consultation with the States? \n \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF WOMEN AND CHILD DEVELOPMENT  \n(SHRIMATI SMRITI ZUBIN IRANI) \n \n \n(a) to (f): The Ministry of Women and Child Development conducted a national mapping exercise of \nChild Care Institutions (CCIs) in 2016, in order to ascertain whether CCIs being run by St ate \nGovernments/UT Administrations under the Juvenile Justice (Care and Protection of Children) Act, \n2015 (JJ Act), are in line with the standards mandated by the JJ Act and Model Rules framed thereunder \nand to institute corrective measures where required. The analysis showed that many CCIs use forms of \ndisciplining children which fall under the ambit of corporal punishment as defined by the JJ Act.  \n \nThe Ministry shared the information with States/UTs drawing their attention towards the findings vide \nletter dated 4th May, 2017. The Ministry stressed upon the need for mandatory monitoring as prescribed \nunder the Juvenile Justice (Child and Protection of Children) Act, 2015 which mandates regular \nmonitoring by Child Welfare Committees, Juvenile Justice Boards  and State Governments . T he \nprimary responsibility in execution of the JJ Act lies with the State/UT Governments.  \n \nThe Chief Secretaries of all the States/UTs were requested to get the inspection conducted under the \nsupervision of District Magistrate s.  The Ministry also issued an advisory to the States and UTs  \nregarding actions required to be taken in case of abuse of children in any Child Care Institutions. \n*** \n  \n\n ",
    "ls|LS17_S1_U_177": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.177 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 03.07.2019 \n \nELECTRIFICATION OF RAILWAY LINES \n \n*177. SHRI UPENDRA SINGH RAWAT:  \nSHRI KAUSHAL KISHORE: \n         \n  Will the Minister of RAILWAYS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government is planning to take up electrification of \nrailway lines in the country; \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof, State/UT-wise; \n \n(c) the details of th e electrification of railway li nes being taken up \npassing through Kurnool distr ict of Andhra Pradesh  along with the \nquantum of funds estimated to be spent thereon; \n \n(d) the details of steps being taken by the Government to \nachieve/ensure the expeditious completion of electrification in \nMaharashtra and Tamil  Nadu, particularly Chennai-Kanyakumari railway \nline; and \n \n(e) the quantum of funds estimated to be spent on the electrification of \nall railway lines in the country, State/UT-wise? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF RAILWAYS AND COMMERCE & INDUSTRY \n \n(SHRI PIYUSH GOYAL) \n \n \n(a) to (e) A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n \n***** \n \n  \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (e) OF STARRED \nQUESTION NO . 177 BY  SHRI UPENDRA SINGH RAWAT AND SHRI KAUSHAL \nKISHORE TO BE ANSWERED IN LOK SABHA ON 03.07.2019 REGARDING \nELECTRIFICATION OF RAILWAY LINES  \n \n(a)    Yes, Sir.  Ministry of Railways has planned to electrify balance Broad \nGauge (BG) routes of Indian Railways, keeping in view the advantages of \nelectrification and to achieve seamless operation on electric traction over entire \nBroad Gauge network.  \n \n(b) & (e) State-wise balance BG rail routes  as on 01.04.2019,  planned for \nelectrification with estimated expenditure are given below:- \nS. \nNo. \nSTATE \nBalance Route Kilometers \nplanned for electrification \nEstimated Expenditure \n` in Crore \n1 Andhra Pradesh 819 767.55 \n2 Arunachal Pradesh 11 10.31 \n3 Assam 2465 2310.14 \n4 Bihar 1052 985.91 \n5 Chandigarh 0 0.00 \n6 Chattisgarh 186 174.31 \n7 Delhi 0 0.00 \n8 Goa 188 176.19 \n9 Gujarat 2567 2405.73 \n10 Haryana 299 280.22 \n11 Himachal Pradesh 0 0.00 \n12 Jammu &Kashmir 135 126.52 \n13 Jharkhand 660 618.54 \n14 Karnataka 3105 2909.93 \n15 Kerala 190 178.06 \n16 Madhya Pradesh 1278 1197.71 \n17 Maharashtra 2446 2292.33 \n--2-- \n\n--2-- \nS. \nNo. \nSTATE \nBalance Route Kilometers \nplanned for electrification \nEstimated Expenditure \n` in Crore \n18 Manipur 13 12.18 \n19 Meghalaya 9 8.43 \n20 Mizoram 2 1.87 \n21 Nagaland 11 10.31 \n22 Odisha 242 226.80 \n23 Puducherry 11 10.31 \n24 Punjab 1331 1247.38 \n25 Rajasthan 3796 3557.51 \n26 Sikkim 0 0.00 \n27 Tamil Nadu 1552 1454.49 \n28 Telengana 497 465.78 \n29 Tripura 216 202.43 \n30 Uttar Pradesh 4380 4104.82 \n31 Uttarakhand 200 187.43 \n32 West Bengal 1149 1076.81 \n \nTotal  28,810   27,000 \n \n(c) Details of un -electrified railway lines  passing through Kurnool District , \nbeing taken up for electrification are as under:- \n(i) Gadwal-Kurnool City-Dhone railway line : It is part of Manmad-Mudkhed-\nDhone electrification project, sanctioned at a cost of ` 664.50 crore.   \n(ii) Nandyal-Sanjamala-Yerraguntla railway line: Electrification of this section \nhas been sanctioned at a cost of `111.48 crore.  \n       --3/…. \n\n--3-- \n \n(d)     In the State of Maharashtra and Tamil Nadu , the electrification works are \nin progress for 2,446 and 1,552 BG Route Kilometers respectively.   \n \nTo expedite electrification of railway lines in the country , including States \nof Maharashtra and Tamil Nadu, steps taken include award of Engineering \nProcurement and Construction (EPC) contracts, better project monitoring \nmechanism, delegati on of more power to field units for award of \ncontracts/sanction of estimates and close monitoring at highest level.   \n \nChennai - Kanniyakumari rail route (via Villupuram & Tiruchchirappalli) has \nalready been electrified. \n \n****** ",
    "ls|LS17_S1_U_178": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF COMMERCE & INDUSTRY \nDEPARTMENT FOR PROMOTION OF INDUSTRY AND INTERNAL TRADE \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 178 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON WEDNESDAY, THE 3RD JULY, 2019. \n \nINFLUX OF CHINESE PRODUCTS \n \n*178.  SHRI SUNIL KUMAR  MONDAL: \n \nWill the Minister of COMMERCE AND INDUSTRY be pleased to state: \n                  वाणिज्य एवं उद्योग मंत्री \n(a)  whether huge influx of Chinese products in the Indian market has affected  the \ndomestic manufacturers;  \n \n(b)  if so, the details thereof; and \n \n(c)  the corrective steps taken/ being taken by the Government in this regard? \n \n \nANSWER \nवाणिज  ् य एवं उद्योग मंत्री (श्री पीयूष गोयल)  \nTHE MINISTER OF COMMERCE & INDUSTRY \n(SHRI  PIYUSH GOYAL) \n \n(a) to (c):  A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n******** \n  \n\n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (c) OF \nTHE LOK SABHA STARRED QUESTION NO. 178  FOR ANSWER ON \nWEDNESDAY, THE 3RD  JULY, 2019.  \n \n \n(a) & (b):  Total imports from China was USD 76.3 billion in 2017 -18, \nwhich decreased to USD70.3 billion in 2018-19. \n \n(c):   The following remedial steps have been taken in this regard: \n \ni. In products where there has been injury to domestic \nindustry, relief has been provided through anti -\ndumping duty on 97 products, countervailing \nmeasures on 3 products  and safeguard duty for 1 \nproduct imposed against China. Details of trade \nremedial measures in force against import from China \nare placed in Annexure I.  \n \nii. Basic Custom Duty has been increased on several \nproducts to protect domestic industry.  \n \niii. Quality control orders are being enforced to check \nsub-standard imports.  \n \niv. The Government through its initiatives such as Make \nin India, Ease of Doing Business, Startup India and \nsectoral schemes/programmes  promotes investment \nin domestic manufacturing and  endeavours to \nimprove the competitiveness of domestic industry.  \n \n***** \n \n  \n\nANNEXURE – I \n \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (c) OF THE \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 178 FOR ANSWER ON 3RD JULY, 2019. \n \nTrade remedial measures in force against imports from China \n \nDetails of cases in which duty is in force \nS.No Country Product(s) Quantum of Duties Imposed Expiry Date of \nDuties \n1 2 3 4 5 \n1 China PR Paracetamol Difference between US $ 787 / \nMT and the landed value of \nimports \n09-07-2019 \n2 China PR Certain Rubber \nChemicals-III \nUS$  0.62 to 1.84/Kg 23.07.2019 \n3 China PR Poly Vinyl \nChloride (PVC) \n(suspension \ngrade) \nUS$ 91.27  per MT to US$ \n147.96 per MT \n12.08.2019 \n4 China PR Ceftriaxone \nSodium Sterile \nUS$ 21.85 per KG 13.08.2019 \n5 China PR Electrical \nInsulators \nUS$ 128 per MT to US 2042 per \nMT \n15.09.2019 \n6 China PR Sulphur Black US$ 766 per MT 17.09.2019 \n7 China PR, Ductile Iron Pipe US$ 127.40 to 139.79 per MT 09-10-2019 \n8 China PR Diclofenac \nSodium \nUS$ 2715 per MT 20.11.2019 \n9 China PR Digital Versatile \nDisc Recordable \n(DVD-R’s) \nUS$ 36.67 per 1000 piece 20.11.2019 \n10 China PR Cable Ties US$ 1.99 per KG to US$ 2.13 \nper KG \n08.12.2019 \n11 China PR Sheet Glass US$ 63 per MT 12.03.2020 \n12 China PR Sodium Citrate US$ 367.59 per Mt 19.05.2020 \n13 China PR USB Flash Drives US $ 3.12 per piece 21.05.2020 \n14 China PR Calculators US $ NIL to US $ 1.22 per piece 28.05.2020 \n15 China PR Hot Rolled flat \nproduct of \nStainless Steel of \n304 Grade \nUS $ 309 Per Metric Ton 04.06.2020 \n16 China PR Vitamin -E US$ 1.77 per KG 09.06.2020 \n17 China PR Nylon Tyre Cord \nFabric-II  \nUS$ 0.52 to 1.10 Per Kg 11.06.2020 \n18 China PR Measuring Tapes US$  1.33/Kg to 2.83/Kg 08.07.2020 \n\n19 China PR O-Acids US$ 4.16-US$8.55 per Kg 12.07.2020 \n20 China PR Compact \nfluorescent lamps \n(CFL) \nUS$ 0.302 per pieces 27.07.2020 \n21 China PR Vitamin-C – I US$ 3.74 per KG 05.08.2020 \n22 China PR Flax Fabrics US$ 0.75 per meter 11.08.2020 \n23 China P