    "ls|LS17_S1_U_27": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF TOURISM \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.*27 \nANSWERED ON 24.06.2019 \n \nPLACES ASSOCIATED WITH BASAVESHWARA AS HERITAGE TOWNS \n \n*27.  SHRI ANNASAHEB SHANKAR JOLLE:  \n \nWill the Minister of TOURISM be pleased to state:  \n \n(a)  whether Shri Basaveshwara has been mentioned as the first \npreacher in the world to have thought about Democracy, \nParliament and Casteless society; and  \n(b)  whether the Government is considering/proposes to \nconsider/declare the places associated with Basaveshwara as \nheritage centres/towns i.e. Basavakalyan Kudal Sangam and \nBasavana Baggewadi in Karnataka and if so, the details \nthereof? \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR TOURISM (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) \n(SHRI PRAHLAD SINGH PATEL) \n \n(a) and (b):  A statement is laid on the table of the House. \n \n********* \n\nSTATEMENT IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) AND (b) OF  LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. *27 ANSWERED ON 24.06.2019 \nREGARDING PLACES ASSOCIATED WITH BASAVESHWARA AS \nHERITAGE TOWNS. \n \n(a) and (b):   The Ministry of Tourism does not have any sch eme \nfor declaring sites/destinations as heritage centres/towns. The \nMinistry, however, under its various scheme guidelines, provides \nCentral Financial Assistance to State Governments/Union Territory \nAdministrations for development of tourism related infras tructure, \nsubject to submission of proposals by the S tate Governments and \ntheir adherence to relevant scheme guidelines.  \n \nNo project has been received by the Ministry for the \ndevelopment of tourism infrastructure in Basavakalyan Kudal \nSangam and Basavana Baggewadi in Karnataka. \n \n********** \n \n \n \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S1_U_28": "������������������� �\n���� � ����������������������������������� �\n�\n��� ������ �\n���������� ��������������� �\n���������������������������� �\n�\n��������������������������������� �\n�\n�� ������� ��������������������� �\n�\n����������������������������������������������������������������\n�������\n�\n�������� ������� ��� ��������������������� ������ ������� ����� ��������\n���������������������������������������������������������������������\n��� �\n�\n�����������������������������������������������������������������������\n�\n������ �\n�\n���� ��������� �� � ������������� ������� ���� ��������\n������ ����������������� ������������������������������ �\n����������������������� ��\n�\n������������������������������������������������������������������������\n��� ����������� ���� ������ ���������� ������������������ ��������� �������\n����������������� �\n�\n��������� ����������� ������������ ���������� ���� ��������� ��� ����� �������\n����������� ��� ������ ������������ ����� ����� ������ ������� ��� �����\n���������������������������������������� �\n�\n�\n��������\n�\n�",
    "ls|LS17_S1_U_29": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF HIGHER EDUCATION \n \nLOK  SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 29 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 24.06.2019 \n \nNew Education Policy \n \n*29.  DR. PRITAM GOPINATHRAO MUNDE: \nSHRI ASADUDDIN OWAISI: \n \n Will the Minister of HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether the Government has announced Draft National Educati on Policy (NEP)-2019, if \nso, the salient features thereof and whether the Government has  taken into confidence all \nstakeholders in this regard and if so, the details thereof; \n \n(b) whether the said Policy has been finalised and if so, the m anner in which the Draft NEP, \n2019 is different from the Draft NEP, 2018 along with the detai ls of amendment included in \nthis policy, the reasons for such amendments; \n \n(c) the details of the funds lik ely to be  sanctioned for imple mentation of the said policy \nacross the country including Tamil Nadu; \n \n(d) whether the Government proposes to implement compulsory thr ee language formula in \nall the States under the said Policy and make Hindi as a compul sory subject in all the States, \nif so, whether some States have opposed this formula and if so,  the details thereof along \nwith the reaction of the Government thereto; \n \n(e) whether the proposed Policy is also employment-oriented, if so, the details thereof and if \nnot, the reasons therefor along with the steps taken/to be take n by the Government for \nimplementation of the said Policy and the time by which it is likely to be implemented; and \n \n(f) whether the Government has taken special steps to carry out  comprehensive reforms in \nthe standard of education in the economically backward areas pa rticularly Bundelkhand \nregion and if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \n \n(SHRI RAMESH POKHRIYAL 'NISHANK') \n \n(a) to (f): A Statement is laid on the table of the House. \n \n************* \n\n \n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO  IN REPLY TO PART (a) to (f) OF THE LOK  \nSABHA STARRED QUESTION NO. 29 FOR REPLY ON 24.06.2019 ASKED BY DR. \nPRITAM GOPINATHRAO MUNDE AND SHRI ASADUDDIN OWAISI  \nREGARDING NEW EDUCATION POLICY \n \n(a) and (b): The Committee for dr aft National Education Policy under the Chairmanship of \nDr. K Kasturirangan has submitted their draft to the Ministry o n 31.05.2019. The draft has \nbeen uploaded in the Ministry of Human Resource Development web s i t e  f o r  s e e k i n g  \ncomments of stakeholders. The National Education Policy will be  finalized by the \nGovernment only after examining all the suggestions/comments of stakeholders.  \n \n(c): The National Education Policy will be accompanied by an im plementation plan \nconsisting of specific initiatives, investments and outcomes. \n \n(d): The 3 Language Formula has been adopted in the National Po licy on Education 1968 \nand reiterated in National Policy on Education 1986. Section 4. 5  o f  t h e  D r a f t  N E P  2 0 1 9  \nemphasizes on the power of language and multilingualism and pro viding education in the \nlocal language/mother tongue in early stages. Further, the vari ous sub-sections of Section \n4.5 of the draft report outline the importance of learning more  languages for enhancing the \ncognitive abilities of children, bilingual approach for those whose language is different from \nthe primary medium of instruction, exposure to three or more la nguages in schools and \nstandardising sign language. Further, while reaffirming the imp ortance of implementing the \nextant Three Language Formula in letter and spirit, the draft r eport proposes for flexibility \nwithin the same regarding the choice of languages. \n \n(e): Draft National Education Policy 2019 inter-alia proposes to integrate vocational \neducation into all educational institutions – schools, colleges and universities and to provide \naccess to vocational education to at least 50% of all learners by 2025, which will be \nfacilitated through collaborati on between educati onal institutions and technical institutions \nand industry. Each educational institution will make a careful choice of a few areas that they \nwould like to offer, based on an analysis of the jobs available in their regions. The focus will \nbe the development of practical s kills as well as the associate d theoretical knowledge along \nwith a broad-based education.  It also proposes a strong undergraduate liberal education with \nintegration of various subjects so as to develop 21st century c apabilities necessary for the \nemployment landscape of the futur e - such as critical thinking,  communication, problem \nsolving, creativity, cultural l iteracy, global outlook, teamwork, ethical reasoning, and social \nresponsibility – which will not only help to develop outstandin g employees but also \noutstanding citizens and communities.  \n \n(f): Currently there are several  ongoing initiatives and scheme s aimed at improving and \nreforming the quality of education in backward regions as well as to address social, gender \nand regional imbalances in educational development. The Draft NEP 2019 also recommends \nthat the initiatives need to be taken to include educationally disadvantaged and Under \nRepresented Groups (URGs) at all stages of education which will  include creation of \nSpecial Education Zones in educationally disadvantaged regions across the country with \ntargeted funding. \n  \n***** ",
    "ls|LS17_S1_U_3": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF AYURVEDA, YOGA & NATUROPATHY, UNANI,\nSIDDHA AND HOMOEOPATHY (AYUSH)\nLOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO.3\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21ST JUNE, 2019\nPOPULARISATION OF AYUSH AND YOGA\n†*3. SHRI KANAKMAL KATARA:\nWill the Minister of AYURVEDA, YOGA AND NATUROPATHY,\nUNANI, SIDDHA AND HOMOEOPATHY (AYUSH) be pleased to state:\n(a) whether the Government is implementing any programme to popularize\nAYUSH system of medicine at the international level;\n(b) if so, the details thereof;\n(c) the steps taken by the Government to encourage people from abroad to visit\nIndia for yoga and spiritual activities; and\n(d) the action being taken by the Government to promote new technology for\nprotection, promotion and processing of herbs under AYUSH?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF STATE(IC) OF THE MINISTRY OF\nAYURVEDA, YOGA & NATUROPATHY, UNANI, SIDDHA AND\nHOMOEOPATHY\n(SHRI SHRIPAD YESSO NAIK)\n(a) to (d) :  A statement is laid on the Table of the House\n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO LOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 3* FOR 21ST JUNE, 2019\n(a) and (b) Under Central Sector Scheme for promotion of International\nCooperation, (IC Scheme), the Ministry of AYUSH undertakes various\nmeasures to promote/ popularize AYUSH systems of medicine across the\nglobe such as organization and participation in International exhibitions/\nconferences/ workshops/ trade fairs, etc.  Incentives are provided to AYUSH\ndrug manufacturers/ entrepreneurs/ A YUSH institutions, etc. for (a)\nparticipating in International exhibitions/ conferences/ workshops/\nseminars/ road shows/ trade fairs, etc. for generating awareness amongst\nthe participating public about the AYUSH systems of medicine and; (b)\nregistration of AYUSH products with regulatory authorities of the foreign\ncountries.\nAs of now, Ministry of AYUSH has signed 18 Country to Country MoUs\nfor Cooperation in field of Traditional Medicine and Homoeopathy. 19 MoUs\nfor undertaking Collaborative Research / Academic collaboration and 13\nMoUs for setting up AYUSH Academic Chairs ha ve been signed with foreign\nUniversities.  The Ministry of AYUSH under its Fellowship Scheme offers 104\nscholarships to the eligible foreign nationals from 98 countries for\nundertaking UG, PG and Ph.D courses in India. 31 AYUSH Information Cell\nhave been set up in 28 countries to disseminate authentic information about\nAYUSH systems of Medicine.\nUnder IC Scheme of the Ministry of AYUSH, Ministry of AYUSH\nprovides onetime non -recurring grant of Rs. 15.00 lakhs per Cell for initial\nsetup followed by a recu rring amount of Rs.5.00 Lakh per cell per annum to\nIndian Mission for its maintenance and to take up the AYUSH promotional\nactivities. There is a provision of financial support upto  Rs. 100.00 lakh  for\nparticipation/ organization of International exhibit ions/ conferences/\nworkshops/ seminars/ road shows/ trade fairs, etc. in India and abroad by\nthe Ministry of AYUSH through Indian Mission/ CII/ FICCI/ ITPO/\nASSOCHAM/ Pharmexcil, etc. Financial support upto Rs.15.00 lakh is\nprovided for organization of Int ernational conferences/ workshops/\n\nseminars, etc. in India on AYUSH Systems by State Governments/\nUniversities/ eminent institutions or organizations etc.\nAs informed by ICCR, International Day of Yoga (IDY) is celebrated in\nforeign countries in coordination with Indian Missions and Posts abroad for\npromotion of Indian Yoga.  ICCR have deployed 86 India - based Yoga\nTeachers in Indian Missions/ Posts and Cultural Centres abroad\n(c) Consequent to adoption of 21 st June as International Day of Yoga\n(IDY), Ministry of AYUSH has been organizing International Yoga\nConferences for the last 04 consecutive years wherein Yoga experts or\nenthusiasts from across the globe are invited to participate.\nMorarji Desai National Institute of Yoga (MDNIY) a nd Institute of Post\nGraduate Teaching & Research in Ayurveda (IPGTRA) have been re -\ndesignated as WHO Collaborative Centres in Traditional Medicine. Ministry of\nAYUSH had launched the Scheme for “Voluntary Certification of Yoga\nProfessionals” developed by Quality Council of India (QCI), on 22 nd June,\n2015 during the 1 st International Conference on Yoga. The Scheme aims to\ncertify the competence level of Yoga professionals through Certification\nProcess.\nAs informed by Ministry of Tourism , Medical and W ellness Tourism\nincluding Yoga and Ayurveda has been recognised as a Niche Tourism\nproduct in order to promote India as a 365 days' destination and attract\ntourists with specific interest.   A \"National Medical and Wellness Tourism\nBoard\" has been constitu ted to provide a dedicated institutional framework\nto take forward the cause of promotion of Medical and Wellness Tourism\nincluding Ayurveda, Yoga, Unani, Siddha and Homeopathy (AYUSH) system\nof medicine.   The Ministry of Tourism offers financial support for Publicity\nand for organising Wellness and Medical Tourism Promotion shows\n/workshop/events/seminar to accredited Medical and Wellness Tourism\nService Providers and Chambers of Commerce, etc.\n\nMedical and Medical attendant visa has been introduced to e ase the\ntravel process of Medical Tourists and the e-tourist visa regime has been\nexpanded to include medical visit . The Ministry of Tourism promotes\nMedical and Wellness Tourism for boosting inflow of foreign tourists in a\nholistic manner, inter alia , by running campaigns in the international\nmarkets under the Incredible India brand line; conducting Road Shows, Know\nIndia Seminars; participating in international tourism fairs and exhibitions\nand supporting events/ seminars / conferences which have focus o n health\nand medical tourism.\n(d) National Medicinal Plants Board (NMPB ) and Department of\nBiotechnology (DBT), Ministry of Scien ce & Technology have entered into a\nMemorandum of Understanding (MoU) for mutual collaboration to explore\nthe possibility of cooperation, convergence & synergy to have a\nbiotechnological intervention in AYUSH sector and to make a platform for\nexchange of information between both the organiz ations. The activities\ncovered for protection and promotion of medicinal plants include u se of\nEcological Niche modelling as developed by DBT on Rare, Endangered  and\nThreatened ( RET) species of medicinal importance for their conservation,\naugmentation, development and cultivation promotion.\nUnder Central Sector Scheme of NMPB on “Conservation, Development\nand Sustainable Management of Medicinal Plants”, the project based support\nis provided for protection of medicinal plants in wild by way of establishi ng\nMedicinal Plant Conservation and Development Area (MPCDA). Under above\nsaid Central Sector Scheme, the support is also provided to\n(JFMCs)/Panchayats/Van Panchayats /Block Management Committees\n(BMCs)/Self Help Groups (SHGs) for creation of facilities ( including\nequipments for value addition through drying, de -stoning, cleaning, grading,\npulverizing, processing, powdering, billeting and packaging, extracting,\nwarehousing, etc. Further, under the NAM scheme of Ministry of AYUSH ,\nthere is also a provision to provide support for setting up of processing\nunits for medicinal plants.\n\nNMPB in collaboration with Regional Remote Sensing Centre West,\nIndian Space Research Organisation, Jodhpur has developed an application\nentitled “Bhuvan e-HERBS” to Geo-tag all the stakeholders and implementing\nagencies on one platform. At present 237 projects have been geo -tagged on\nBhuvan e-HERBS.",
    "ls|LS17_S1_U_30": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF CULTURE\n \nLOK SABHA\n \nSTARRED QUESTION  NO. \n*\n30\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON \n24.06\n.201\n9\n \n \n \n \n                 \nCONSERVATION OF\n \nMONUMENTS\n \n \n      \n \n \n*\n30\n \n \nSHRI  \nRAMCHARAN BOHRA\n:\n \nSHRI\n \nRAVIJ PRATAP RUDY  :\n \n \nWill the Minister of CULTURE be pleased to state:\n \n \n \n(a)\n \nwhether the Government has taken adequate steps for the conservation\n \nand preservation of archaeological/heritage sites and monuments in the \ncountry;\n \n \n(b)\n \nif so, the \ndetails thereof along with the details of archaeological/heritage \nsites\n \nand monuments taken up for maintenance and restoration during \neach of the  last three years and the current year in the country, \nmonument/site\n-\nwise and State/UT\n-\nwise including Bihar;\n \n(c)\n \nthe details of funds allocated and expenditure incurred in this regard\n \nduring the last three years and the current year, monument/site\n-\nwise and\n \nyear\n-\nwise;\n \n(d)\n \n \n \n(e)\n \n \nthe details of sites which the Government intends to take up for\n \nrestoration in the \nnear future along with the time by which the\n \nconservation work of these sites \nis\n \nlikely to be completed; and\n \nwhether the Government has prescribed  certain procedure to provide\n \ncontracts for development of such sites and if so, the details thereof?\n \n \nANSWE\nR\n \n \nMINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) FOR CULTURE AND \nTOURISM \n \n \n(\nPRAHLAD \nSINGH \nPATEL\n)\n \n \n \n \n(a)\n \nto (e)\n \nA statement is laid on the table of the House\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (a) TO (e) OF THE LOK \nSABHA STARRED QUESTION NO. *\n30\n \nFOR \n24.06.2019\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n(a)\n \n&\n \n(b)\n \n \n \n \n \nYes Sir, Archaeological Survey of India (ASI) has 3691 Nos. of \nprotected monuments of national importance in the country including the \nstate of Bihar.  The conservation work of protected monuments is a \ncontinuous process and exec\nuted by the ASI regularly depending upon \nthe requirements of different sites and availability of resources.  Due to \nregular care by the ASI they are in a \nfine\n \nstate of preservation.    \n \n \n \n(c)\n \n \n \nThe details of funds allocated and expenditure incurred for \nconservation \nof protected monuments in the country during the last three years and \nallocation for the current financial year are given in the annexure\n.\n \n \n \n \n(d)\n \n \nBeing a continuous process the need based conservation work on \nprotected monuments is attended \nregularly keeping them in presentable \ncondition.\n \n \n \n \n(e)\n \n \nThe main thrust is for conservation of the protected monuments.  The \ndevelopment works related to public amenities and for enhancing the \nvisitors’ experience are executed following standard \nguidelines and \nprocedure. Some of the development works have been a\nssigned to \nGovernment of India P\nublic sector undertakings\n \n(PSU’s)\n.\n \n \n \n \n\n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (c) OF THE LOK SABHA  STARRED QUESTION \nNO. 30 FOR 24.06.2019\n \n \nSTATE/UT\n-\nWISE DETAILS OF ALLOCATION OF FUNDS AND EXPENDITURE INCURRED DURING THE \nLAST THREE YEARS AND ALOCATION FOR CURRENT FINANCIAL YEAR  FOR CONSERVATION, \nPRESERVATION AND ENVIRONMENTAL DEVELOPMENT  OF MONUMENTS\n \n                                         \n                                                                                            \n(Amount Rs. in Lakhs)\n \nSl. \nNo\n.\n \nName of \nState/UT\n \nCircle / Branch\n \nTotal \nNumber of \nMonuments \n2016\n-\n17\n \n2017\n-\n18\n \n2018\n-\n19\n \n2019\n-\n20\n \n(Vote on \nAccount)\n \nAllocation\n \nExp.\n \nAllocation\n \nExp.\n \n \nAllocation\n \nExp.\n \nAllocation\n \n1.\n \nUttar Pradesh\n \nAgra Circle\n \n745\n \n940.00\n \n940.00\n \n850.00\n \n850.00\n \n860.00\n \n858.61\n \n400.00\n \nLucknow Circle\n \n689.50\n \n688.5